        "ufo (URL utilities, TypeScript under src/, every module re-exported from src/index.ts) can add or overwrite query parameters with withQuery(input, query) and read them with getQuery(input), both in src/utils.ts, but it has no way to drop parameters selectively. Add, beside withQuery, and export from the package entry src/index.ts a function filterQuery(input: string, predicate: (key: string, value: string | string[]) => boolean): string that returns input keeping only the query parameters for which predicate returns true. Its rules: an input that contains no \"?\" is returned exactly as given (filterQuery(\"file:///C:/x\", () => false) is \"file:///C:/x\", not normalized). Otherwise predicate is called once for each key of getQuery(input), in that order, with the decoded key and the value getQuery gives it (a string, or an array of strings for a repeated key, so a repeated key is kept or dropped as a whole), and the URL is rebuilt the way withQuery rebuilds it: filterQuery(input, () => true) equals withQuery(input, {}) for every input that contains \"?\"; protocol, auth, host, path and fragment are kept as withQuery keeps them; the kept parameters are encoded as withQuery encodes them; and the \"?\" is left out when no parameter remains. Examples: filterQuery(\"/foo?bar=1&baz=2\", (key) => key !== \"bar\") is \"/foo?baz=2\"; filterQuery(\"/foo?bar=1\", (key) => key !== \"bar\") is \"/foo\"; filterQuery(\"/foo?a=1&a=2&bar=3#frag\", (key) => key !== \"bar\") is \"/foo?a=1&a=2#frag\"; filterQuery(\"https://example.com/p?q=%20x&bar=1\", (key, value) => value !== \"1\") is \"https://example.com/p?q=+x\". What must not change: every existing export keeps its name and its behaviour for every input, in particular withQuery, getQuery, parseQuery, stringifyQuery, parseURL and stringifyParsedURL return exactly what they return today (for example withQuery(\"/x?bar\", {}) stays \"/x?bar\", withQuery(\"/foo?\", {}) stays \"/foo\" and withQuery(\"/p?x=1&&bar=2&\", {}) stays \"/p?x=1&bar=2\"), and getQuery and parseQuery still return an object that does not inherit from Object.prototype and still ignore __proto__ and constructor keys. The tests are vitest files under test/, with the query utilities in test/query.test.ts. Add regression tests for filterQuery to the project's existing test suite. The project's tests run with npx vitest run --typecheck (npm test runs pnpm lint first; on the unchanged base under this install, prettier already reports src/punycode.ts, so lint is not part of the required test command).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npx vitest run --typecheck\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npx vitest run --typecheck) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes."
